Fiat presents the 500l the 1960  cinquecento revisited this baby is a  retro style design conceived for long  trips the original 500 played the same  rule in Italy as the beetle did in  Germany says our reviewer mantas the  compact car that mobilised the masses in  2007 the Italians remembered what a  pretty card was and decided to relaunch  it back in the 60s the L version stood  for luxury today it means March but  actually the new 500 L is more a Fiat  Punto Montes is going to put it through  its paces and see what it can do fast  echoes the clarinet arena Alice can the  design is a winner the round forms are  pleasing to the eye and like various  chrome trim details paid tribute to the  old Fiat 500  from the outside it looks like the 500l  has lots of trunk space  it holds 400 litres but a German  Shepherd would hardly have enough space  for a nice ride but it will hold plenty  of potatoes bags and bags of them the  seats can be folded down in two ways  producing 1310 liters of cargo space  enough for 5000 spuds montes takes the  500l onto a country road where it cuts a  good figure  the suspension provides a good feeling  for the road without being uncomfortable  the steering is a bit soft with a slack  response but it's a safe and secure ride  nonetheless  montes likes the front field of vision  and the addition of windows in front of  the door the back window is narrow  making it hard to judge where the car  ends when in Reverse all too often the  500 ELLs interior has lots of pleasing  vintage style details little design  touches can be found everywhere the  sat-nav is mounted on the dashboard  montes would have preferred it  integrated into the stereo system the  radio provides good sound the Lounge  versions interior is extra stylish the  back seats even have tree tables for  longer trips the seats are comfortable  but too short taller folk will find  their legs hanging in the air even  montes not so big has 10 unsupported  centimetres of leg with it's 88  kilowatts of power the Fiat 500l is not  a powerhouse but it can zip along nicely  for the top speed of 183 kilometers an  hour the 1.6 liter diesel version  consumes 4.8 liters of fuel per 100  kilometers not bad at all long trips  aren't unnecessarily expensive the  lounge edition with the big diesel  engine costs 22,000 150 euros  yeah I'm Martha says the engine doesn't  feel at home at low revs around 1500 per  minute it starts to work better at about  1800 revs and then it moves forward well  the lounge version also has a panoramic  glass roof bat and the large windows  mean the interior is flooding with light  and also provide the passengers with a  good view  mantas really likes the panorama roof  and the incredible feeling for space it  gives the Fiat 500l so the 500l has more  to offer than good looks our tester  summary the Fiat 500l s driving  qualities are convincing and the price  is acceptable but to be honest it  doesn't have much in common with the  real Fiat 500 apart from some design  elements families will be interested to  hear that the Fiat 500l also comes in a  7 seat version with a third row of seats
 

